WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.340
All right, hi and welcome
to Robotics Today.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.160
The My name is Luca Carlone.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.960
And I'm excited to introduce
Dr. Raia Hadsell as our speaker

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.600
today.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.240
Raia is the director of
robotics and DeepMind.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.480
Her research focuses on the
challenge of continual learning

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.400
for AI agents and robots.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.280
And she's proposed
popular approaches

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.250
such as quality distillation,
progressive nets,

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.950
and elastic reconsolidation
to solve the problem

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.890
of catastrophic forgetting.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.130
Let me say that, Raia had
a quite unique career path.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.890
After an undergraduate degree
in religion and philosophy

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.800
from Reed College, she started
to be interested in computer

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.850
science and ended up doing a
PhD with Yann LeCun at NYU,

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.820
focusing on machine
learning for robotics.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.490
She knew also post-doc at
CMU working with Drew Bagnell

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:54.900
and Martial Hebert.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.670
And then became a
research scientist

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:00.510
in the Vision and Robotics
Group at SRI International.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.690
Finally, she joined
DeepMind in 2014

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.780
to pursue new solutions
for artificial general

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.775
intelligence.

00:01:08.775 --> 00:01:11.370
Raia's Work is being
widely recognized.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.070
Her thesis about Learning
Long-range Vision

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.580
for Off-road Robots was awarded
the Outstanding Dissertation

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.040
award in 2009.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.550
She's on the executive
boards of ICLR,

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.800
Women in Machine
Learning, and CoRL.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.840
She's a Fellow of the European
Lab on Learning System

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.866
and serves as a CIFAR advisor.

00:01:31.866 --> 00:01:33.480
Raia, thanks for being here.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.820
The stage is yours.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.602
Thank you very much, Luca.

00:01:37.602 --> 00:01:39.060
Looking forward to
giving the talk.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.055
Thanks for the invitation
and for the introduction.

00:01:43.055 --> 00:01:43.680
Let's see here.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.980
I'm going to talk a
little bit about what

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.920
I think would be a few
different directions where

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.560
we could go to--

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.210
a little bit of a different way
to think about machine learning

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.050
and robotics.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.150
But let me start first
with Deep Learning.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.260
So as we all know
now, Deep Learning

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.830
is powerful because
we've got big data

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.530
and we've got these
wonderful architectures,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.040
these Deep Learning
models that really scale

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.050
very well to fit the data set.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.210
And there's always
sort of this notion

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.960
that we've got a lot of data
and we've got this big model.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.690
And when the two scale
together, then we

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:29.180
can really achieve
impressive things.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.330
This is well supported
by large-scale compute

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.970
and by end-to-end optimization.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.940
Now, you can think about Deep
RL as looking a little bit

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.050
the same.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.890
Sorry, microphone problems.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:48.080
Again, we are thinking
about a certain balance

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.450
that we might imagine
between an agent

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:52.670
that we're training
and an environment

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.350
that we're learning in.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.810
With the difference
being that, of course,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.390
in Deep Reinforcement
Learning, then we're

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:02.180
thinking about the
agent is actually

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.190
generating its own
data sets that it's

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:07.580
learning from by taking
actions in the environment

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.000
and learning from the
observations that come back

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.150
in order to maximize
your reward.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:18.080
So, yes, the agent
built it's own data set.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:24.650
And with this sort of simple
view of it, in a lot of ways,

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:28.130
we've been able to come up with
a lot of interesting things.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:33.170
We've been able to
optimize over behaviors

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:36.080
and discovered
things that are not

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.500
obvious and not easy
to directly encode,

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:42.650
such as behavior, of
course, of this agent that's

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.640
learning to play an Atari game.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.180
And we can-- using the almost
exactly the same approach,

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:53.480
we can also see impressive
behaviors emerging when

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.150
we train an agent
to, for instance,

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.310
simply maximize its
forward velocity.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:04.010
So this 56 dimensional
humanoid model

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:07.850
can be taught to
run across a terrain

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.880
and jump across gaps in
quite an impressive way.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.430
This agent was optimized
for a very long time

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.149
until it could solve this
task really, really well.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:18.200
And why does this work?

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.690
Again, because
we've got an agent

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.110
that is powerful enough that
it can fit to that environment

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.250
that we've given it.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.365
It's essentially memorizing
the entire environment

00:04:28.365 --> 00:04:32.570
is why it optimized it so well.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.860
And this is impressive.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:35.360
It's fun.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.080
It's interesting.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.900
But we want to go a
little bit beyond this.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.990
We want to move AI research
beyond the simple picture

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.260
of having an agent
and an environment,

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:50.240
and where the agent can fully
fit to that environment.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.080
Instead, I would
say that we want

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.580
to agents that are actually much
smaller than the environments

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.070
that they learn, such
that there's no hope

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:03.560
that that agent can actually
fit the whole environment.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.030
Why would we want that?

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:10.580
Why would we want
agents that are dwarfed

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:12.950
by the size, the
complexity, or the richness

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:17.060
of the environment, such that
the capacity of the agent

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:20.430
is not sufficient to
model that environment?

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:21.650
Well, we want this--

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.740
it's important because it's
the driver of, I would argue,

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:28.390
many wonderful things that we
look for in our research that

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.620
should transfer.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:34.070
So only by having an
agent that does not

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:38.840
have sufficient capacity to just
learn the entire environment,

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.300
only by having to that
imbalance balance do

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.040
we need to become
better at things

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:49.690
like transfer and generalization
and also fast adaptation.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:51.640
If the environment
is small, the agents

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.960
can just learn the whole thing.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.570
But instead, we can also
have continual learning.

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:01.710
There's no need for
continual learning

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:04.300
if you can observe the
entire environment,

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.040
you can learn at all.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.370
But if we have this
imbalance, then

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.380
this can drive
continual learning,

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.170
can also drive causal reasoning.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.450
Causal reasoning
comes from needing

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:19.860
to have more efficient
ways of understanding

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.740
the relationship and the
dynamics of environment that

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:26.090
can't come from simple--

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:30.290
from memorizing-- from
observing everything.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:31.505
And abstract concepts.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:39.400
So first question, how do we
make an environment complex?

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.430
What does that even mean?

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.740
Is an Atari environment complex?

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.960
Is a humanoid control
environment complex?

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.620
Well, we can think about it
in a few different dimensions

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:54.130
of complexity.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:55.860
So first of all,
the observations

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.150
that an agent takes
in an environment,

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:02.300
real data has much
more complexity

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.860
than in a simulation.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:08.600
So although, I use
simulation in my research,

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.030
I think that it's
important to recognize

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:19.400
the value of real data for the
noise and the complexity that

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:20.930
is expressed there.

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:23.360
There's also partial
observability.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:29.250
So of course, if you can see
everything in an environment,

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:32.210
then it's definitely not
going to be as complex

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:35.005
as if I can only see through
a bit of a soda straw

00:07:35.005 --> 00:07:38.100
or through a limited
view over time.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:41.660
There's also multiple
sensory modalities.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:45.800
So if there is not
only vision, but also

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:51.260
audio, touch information, et
cetera, in the environment,

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.045
then it will be more complex.

00:07:53.045 --> 00:07:55.970
You can also look at the
dynamics of an environment.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:08:02.040
Things like interaction and
contact having multiple agents.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:06.840
This greatly multiplies
the complexity.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.370
And then you can think
about the world itself.

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:13.050
So a lot of research
environments that we look at

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:17.040
or task environments for
training robots or agents

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.410
tend to be very short lived.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.990
We think about maybe
in an environment

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.330
where we can perfectly reset
things and have episodes

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:26.640
that last very--

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.740
on the order of seconds.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.450
Instead, we can think
about the length

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:35.760
of time of the life of
our agents, the experience

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:36.659
of the agents.

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:38.100
And we can think
about worlds that

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:42.990
are persistent where changes
made by one agent at one time

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:44.890
would persist through.

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:50.040
And those changes would be
evident to another agent

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:52.570
at another time.

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:55.180
All right, so let's turn
to how an agent can learn

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:57.650
in a very complex environments.

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:02.810
So one way is through learning
with multiple objectives.

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:07.030
So one way, I believe, to
deal with the fact of having

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:11.860
a lower capacity agent
and a more complex world

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:16.060
is to use multiple reinforcement
learning objectives

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:19.240
for instance, multiple rewards,
constraints, penalties.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:21.220
There should be a
rich scope of things

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:24.250
that an agent is trying
to achieve in any given

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:27.370
environment.

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:29.980
There can also be--

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:34.030
of course, reward is not the
only way in which we can learn.

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:37.690
We can also include supervised
learning and unsupervised

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:40.750
learning, which is all
of the parts of the cake,

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:42.550
as my advisor would say--

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:45.700
former advisor would say.

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:51.130
Another approach is to learn
with many tasks or skills.

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:54.080
So rather than learning
a single skill,

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.830
such as running with
forward velocity

00:09:56.830 --> 00:10:00.730
to the end of the track as that
humanoid was trained to do,

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:03.520
instead learning to do many
different things in one

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:08.890
environment is a
great way for an agent

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:12.880
to learn some of the
different notions of transfer,

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.720
of abstract concepts,
of causality, et cetera.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:19.360
And then the last point
that I would make here

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:21.520
is that learning with
humans is something

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:25.780
that we could do more of to
enable agents to learn, even

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:28.820
in these more
complex environments.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:31.610
And that can include
learning from demonstration

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:34.430
or learning through
imitation, for instance,

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:38.390
from the third
person perspective.

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:44.930
Simple observation, I
think that the agents that

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:50.750
can be taught just to
learn from observing others

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:53.125
in a complex world,
this can be valuable.

00:10:53.125 --> 00:10:54.500
I think about this
every time I'm

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.000
out in the world
with other humans

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.050
and watching them and realizing,
I don't need to imitate them.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:02.240
I learn through simply
observing what they do

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.610
and the outcomes
of their behaviors.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:08.250
And through language as well.

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:10.850
So these are all sorts of
areas, this is very general.

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:12.545
But I just think
that these are--

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:19.790
that we as the
research community

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:23.270
is spending a lot of
time on optimizing

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:26.900
for smaller domain and
maybe simpler approaches,

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:30.140
when really we need to embrace
the complexity of agents

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:33.860
as well as the environments
that they learn.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:37.970
So let me present a
couple of research results

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:43.260
that we've had
recently at DeepMind.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:44.780
So the first one--

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:48.920
and both of these methods
are about imitation.

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:52.440
But imitation used in
very different ways.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:58.160
So imitation can be used as
a source of data as a way

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.695
to learn the fundamentals
movement, the fundamentals

00:12:01.695 --> 00:12:02.195
of control.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:08.870
Or it can be used as sort of the
other end of learning in order

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:12.740
to give a demonstration
or to demonstrate

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:14.970
a task that needs to be done.

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:18.500
So I like to think about
these two different sides

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:21.860
of using expert data.

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:24.860
One at the beginning to
learn representations.

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:28.560
And one at the end
to demonstrate goals.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.930
So the first work is
about using mimicry

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:36.770
to learn basic motor control.

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:43.350
All right, so why do we want
to learn reusable skills?

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:49.980
Well, learning from scratch
can be data efficient.

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:54.410
I'm just showing here this old
parkour video that probably

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:56.030
people have seen before.

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:59.870
The internet seemed
to like this video.

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:08.420
And this showed some
impressively robust control,

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:11.090
behavior and skills.

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:17.300
But we do end up in a regime
where this is data inefficient

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:22.640
and where we don't have
a lot of constraint

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:27.050
of the behavior of
the agent over time.

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:33.200
So exploration is limited
and transfer is limited.

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:35.580
So how can we learn
reusable skills?

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:40.100
Well, we could-- there are
unsupervised skill discovery

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:41.660
methods.

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:44.400
You could learn from
a lot of simple tasks.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.010
For instance, via
the method called

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.650
scheduled auxiliary control.

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:50.240
Or you could use
demonstrations, which

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:51.680
is what this work is based on.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:54.080
Or specifically
do joint training

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:58.470
with tasks and demonstrations.

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:02.660
So let me start by mentioning
neural probabilistic motor

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:08.270
primitives, or
otherwise called NPMP.

00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:17.900
And the idea here is to train
an expert policy by tracking

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:20.690
kinematic motion capture clip.

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:24.080
And then to force
the policy to be

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:28.470
bottlenecked through a latent
layer that can then be reused.

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:29.930
So basically,
we're going to take

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:36.500
some set of clips here of
different types of human motion

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:39.140
coming from human
motion capture.

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:44.790
And we're going to
learn to predict--

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:54.580
we're going to learn to
encode the future state.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:57.640
Go through a latent state,
which we can interpret

00:14:57.640 --> 00:14:59.860
as being a latent intention.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:02.350
And that's going to then
go to a decoder, which

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:04.960
will produce an action.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:07.140
We also feed in
the present state

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.640
the proprioception
of the current robot.

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:12.640
And the output action
tries to imitate

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.680
the next state of these clips.

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.390
So basically, we're
trying to learn

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:21.970
to follow each of these over
time by controlling a human--

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:26.530
by controlling a
humanoid figure.

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:33.480
And the key thing is that
this latent intention

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:38.245
is used to develop this
latent embedding Zt.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:45.810
We can then reuse
exactly the same decoder.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:51.210
Now we've learned how to go
from an input in the state

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.700
in the embedding Zt--

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:57.520
we've learned to go from
that directly to action.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:59.670
So now this becomes
the action space.

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:04.070
And we can learn a task policy
that's going to control this.

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:07.680
So now given an RL
environment, given

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:11.740
some tasks that we
want to do from reward,

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:16.680
then we can reuse the decoder
as a low-level skill module.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:20.240
So we're going to take an
observation from this task

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:22.350
space, whatever that
is, and then we're

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:28.380
going to learn how to create
an action in that space.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:30.880
It's not necessarily a
lower dimensional space.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:34.500
In this case, the action
space, 56 dimensional

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:37.980
and the embedding space
the 60 dimensional.

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:41.100
But that embedding
space, at least,

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:45.420
has within it all of the
different types of motion

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:48.608
that we might expect a
humanoid character to do.

00:16:48.608 --> 00:16:50.400
Does not have a lot of
laying on the ground

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:53.070
and flailing around because
that set of expert clips

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:56.220
does not contain that
type of behavior.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.400
Instead, it has productive
movement locomotion,

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:03.540
sitting, turning, standing,
jumping, et cetera.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:07.950
So this is a really
nice and efficient way

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:11.640
of learning low-level
motor behavior

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:16.480
and then being able to use it
for reinforcement learning.

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:20.819
So the work in
this paper in comic

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:23.430
instead says, this is a
little bit inefficient

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:25.950
to do these things in
two separate spaces.

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:28.050
Let's instead put
it all together

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:31.120
into one RL environment.

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:36.110
So now we're going to
take these both together,

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:38.780
we're going to say, I'm going
to take these expert clips

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:41.580
and I'm going to
try to imitate them.

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:43.670
And I'm also going
to take observations

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:46.320
from tasks that I'm
learning at the same time.

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:50.300
For instance, go through
this obstacle course.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:53.700
And I'm going to
push both these--

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:57.560
I'm going to learn to use Z. At
the same time, this embedding

00:17:57.560 --> 00:18:01.250
space as a latent
intention space, which

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:02.750
tries to do imitation.

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:05.240
And also as a
high-level action space

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:07.280
to fulfill my task policy.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:10.760
And I'm going to reward
both the imitation

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:14.750
and I'm going to reward the task
using quite different rewards.

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:18.590
One is a reward that
comes from following

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:25.640
each joint position of the mocap
data we're trying to track.

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:29.540
And the other one is a
fresh reward from velocity.

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:35.790
So the research
questions that come--

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:37.470
we sort of got us
working together,

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:38.520
the two different parts.

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:40.170
Put them together
and said, let's just

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:42.810
train the whole thing at
the same time, co-training

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:44.950
and mimicry.

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:47.790
But then we wanted
to say, well, how

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:51.660
do the different architectures
we could use compare here?

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:54.050
How do they compare in terms
of the tracking performance?

00:18:54.050 --> 00:18:56.340
How do they compare in
terms of reusability?

00:18:56.340 --> 00:19:01.030
And how well does the joint
training approach work?

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:04.680
So in order to evaluate
this, we use a motion

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:09.360
capture data set from CMU--

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.460
from the CMU motion
capture data set

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:13.990
and the humanoid
model, like I said,

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:16.320
with 56 degrees of freedom.

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:19.740
And in each episode, we
sample a starting point

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:21.420
from a lot of different clips.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:27.510
And use a reward similar
to the DeepMimic paper

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:29.670
where we have terms
that are weighted

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:33.248
to match different
positions to interpretation.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:45.760
We trained this using
on-policy Rl using V-MPO.

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:48.100
The links are there
for more information

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:50.740
about these
different approaches.

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:54.880
And using KL regularization
of the skill embedding space--

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:57.850
just that latent space,
which is important to make

00:19:57.850 --> 00:19:58.903
this work well.

00:19:58.903 --> 00:20:00.820
And then we tried out
some different low-level

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:01.750
architectures.

00:20:01.750 --> 00:20:05.320
So how do we want to
have that decoder--

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:09.890
that action decoder that
becomes the motor module?

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:12.580
How do we want to--

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:15.380
and how do we want
to represent that?

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:16.850
So I looked at an MLP.

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:18.370
We looked at LSTM.

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:21.430
Mixture of Gaussians or
product of Gaussians.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:24.580
But states which have
all been used in previous

00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:29.030
works with different success.

00:20:29.030 --> 00:20:32.140
We just wanted to
compare against these.

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:35.560
And then so we can look
at different motion

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:38.290
capture data sets and
how they are valuable.

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:40.330
So there are two small ones.

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:43.030
One with just walking,
one with just running,

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:46.960
one that has more
larger locomotion.

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:50.470
A lot of different clips there
something up to 40 minutes

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:51.003
of data.

00:20:51.003 --> 00:20:52.420
And then we have
a large sort of--

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:58.750
larger set of clips that has
much longer sequences in it

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:03.370
and a lot more motion, which
is 3 and 1/2 hours long.

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:05.800
For the transfer for
the joint training,

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:08.810
then we looked at a
couple of different tasks.

00:21:08.810 --> 00:21:13.450
First, we have a go
to go to dot task--

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:16.960
go to target task.

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:19.990
And then two different
parkour type of tasks,

00:21:19.990 --> 00:21:24.490
where the humanoid needs
to traverse gaps or wall

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:27.460
obstacles.

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:30.610
These tasks have a dense
reward and they have

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:32.710
egocentric visual observations.

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:34.660
So these aren't done
from state information,

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:37.210
because they're actually
done from visual information.

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:39.370
The proprioception
is available as well.

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:47.290
And we also specifically
looked at a couple of tasks

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:49.600
that we knew were
not represented

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:55.150
in the data of the
original data set.

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:57.430
So being able to get
up from laying down

00:21:57.430 --> 00:22:03.280
is something that's not
present in the motion capture

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:03.817
data sets.

00:22:03.817 --> 00:22:05.650
So we knew that this
is something that would

00:22:05.650 --> 00:22:08.380
need to be learned separately.

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:10.780
And the other one was catching
an object, which is not

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:12.310
represented in the data set.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:20.380
So when we look at just learning
the tracking performance,

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:24.700
learning to track the
motion capture data

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:32.110
set, then we can see that the
best architecture is the LSTM.

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:35.500
So this performs well across all
of the different data regimes

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:37.720
whether or not we have
a small amount of data

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:40.210
or the very largest set.

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:42.990
The MLP also performed
well in all settings.

00:22:49.280 --> 00:22:51.620
Take a look at this
tracking performance

00:22:51.620 --> 00:22:53.960
that is learned in
joint with the task.

00:22:53.960 --> 00:22:57.390
And if we just look at
the tracking performance,

00:22:57.390 --> 00:22:59.960
this is the largest data set.

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:02.980
And we can imitate various
walking and running behaviors.

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:07.940
So the character in silver
is the human motion capture,

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:12.920
and the one in gold is the one
that the agent is controlling.

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:16.460
And probably you saw
that little glitch there

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:19.130
where it just
decides, I'm not going

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:22.470
to try to do that
fancy spin that's

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:24.230
done in motion capture data.

00:23:24.230 --> 00:23:33.070
And the agent sort of skips past
that quite opportunistically.

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:36.790
But in general, we see
that all of the behaviors

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:39.820
can be robustly followed,
including walking backwards,

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:41.330
dancing, et cetera.

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:46.950
Now, if we look at transfer.

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:49.770
So here we have
transfer to the go

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:54.060
to target task, the walls
task, and the gaps task.

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:57.810
Starting with
low-level controllers

00:23:57.810 --> 00:23:59.970
that are trained on either
the walking, running,

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:02.250
the locomotion of
large data set.

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:03.910
And we see that
all of these cases,

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:06.960
even though the LSTM did the
best in the actual tracking

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:13.310
test, then it's the MLP
that does decidedly better.

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:15.410
And this worked across--

00:24:15.410 --> 00:24:19.970
the MLP works across almost
every one of the tests

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:23.630
that we did for that transfer.

00:24:23.630 --> 00:24:30.260
The LSTM low-level
policies are probably

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:32.450
less reliant on the
embedding space.

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:37.010
They're probably memorizing
the data a little bit more,

00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:39.620
whereas the MLP is forced
to stay a little bit more

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:42.500
tied, a little bit more
reliant on the embedding space.

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:44.525
So it's going to do
better for transfer.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:55.450
Here's a video showing
one of the models

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:57.750
traverse an obstacle
course with gaps.

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:00.700
This train simultaneously
with the agent.

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:09.030
So the gaps task is not
present in the training regime.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.145
And it does do quite
well on the training.

00:25:14.617 --> 00:25:15.950
Let's take a look one more time.

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:20.090
Now compared with the original
parkour agents that I showed

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:25.310
you that is galloping
across this gaps task,

00:25:25.310 --> 00:25:27.500
this agent does not
do quite as well.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:31.640
That's because it's actually
constrained by the latent

00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:32.690
space.

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:36.585
Now, training on this task is no
longer training all the way end

00:25:36.585 --> 00:25:37.190
to end.

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:43.640
But instead, the training
stops at the latent embedding.

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:46.880
And therefore, you don't get
quite that full optimization

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:52.940
that you get if you only train
for this task from scratch.

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:54.980
On the other hand,
this was able to be

00:25:54.980 --> 00:26:00.470
trained in a matter of
minutes rather than days.

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:06.500
And it uses a bit less energy.

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:09.125
And in terms of the--

00:26:09.125 --> 00:26:16.160
we look at the actions
that are taken, and is

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:19.940
a bit more constrained in
terms of what it learns.

00:26:19.940 --> 00:26:25.340
So we see this transfer
as being important,

00:26:25.340 --> 00:26:28.130
even though it doesn't get
to quite the same performance

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:33.390
as the original parkour agent
that can run through very

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:33.890
quickly.

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:43.690
I think I'm going to move on
to the next piece of research

00:26:43.690 --> 00:26:45.820
that I wanted to talk about.

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:48.460
There's more details in
this paper, which I would

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:49.780
encourage people to look at.

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:52.000
There's a longer
video and website

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:55.090
that shows more of the results,
some of the co-training

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:57.460
reports that I'm not
going to go into here.

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:06.030
So as I said, there
are different ways

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:08.760
to use expert data.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:10.380
One way in which
I just showed is

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:12.690
to use expert data
as a starting point.

00:27:12.690 --> 00:27:15.840
Expert data has a
way to quickly--

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:20.190
more efficiently get to a
low-level representation

00:27:20.190 --> 00:27:22.270
that has been more reusable.

00:27:22.270 --> 00:27:26.970
The other way in which we
would want to use demonstration

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:31.920
for robotics is to use it as
the end point to give a targets.

00:27:31.920 --> 00:27:36.270
To demonstrate a task and then
being able to quickly execute

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:37.510
that task.

00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:41.370
So that's what this
next book focuses on.

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:45.420
The title of this is
Manipulator-Independent

00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:48.400
Representation Learning
for Visual Imitation.

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:57.640
So the work focuses on
third-person imitation

00:27:57.640 --> 00:27:59.920
for robotics manipulation.

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:03.505
And the challenge here
is imitation from pixels.

00:28:06.410 --> 00:28:13.240
So this video-- this
sort of crude rendering

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:16.210
shows a teleoperation
that we might

00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:19.330
want to do where we
demonstrate moving a block

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:22.930
and stacking it on
top of another block.

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:25.810
What we would like to do is
be able to take something

00:28:25.810 --> 00:28:29.500
from this simple
information that's shown

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:32.080
and be able to replicate
this with a real robot

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:33.400
in the real world.

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:36.550
Or even better, being able
to demonstrate something

00:28:36.550 --> 00:28:42.290
like this with a human hand and
then repeat it with a robot.

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:47.000
And it's important to say
that we want to be able to--

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:52.040
we don't want to have to use
the teleoperation of the robot

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:54.830
directly but be able to
do this imitation just

00:28:54.830 --> 00:28:57.680
from third-person imitation.

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:02.120
So not have task rewards,
not have proprioception,

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:02.945
not have action.

00:29:02.945 --> 00:29:04.940
But simply have
the visual system

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:07.720
and be able to
imitate from that.

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:10.360
And the challenge is, of course,
that we have this domain gap,

00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:16.085
we have sort of in simulation,
things that look one way.

00:29:16.085 --> 00:29:17.710
And then in the real
world, then things

00:29:17.710 --> 00:29:19.280
are going to look
quite different.

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:24.530
Or we could have human
hands, we could have grippers

00:29:24.530 --> 00:29:27.520
that could move things around.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:34.630
And so we really need to learn
at extreme levels of invariance

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:38.620
or robustness if we want
to be able to imitate

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:42.416
from this type of demonstration.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:48.760
So the approach here--

00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:53.230
I will point to this figure
here where what we see

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:58.450
is a hand that is demonstrating
a stacking of one block on top

00:29:58.450 --> 00:29:59.950
of another block.

00:29:59.950 --> 00:30:01.930
Human is here doing that.

00:30:01.930 --> 00:30:09.020
And we want to send the sort of
first way points along the way.

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:17.100
We want to send that through
an encoder to a representation

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:18.890
space-- to a feature space.

00:30:18.890 --> 00:30:22.130
And then use that as a
goal for an imitating agent

00:30:22.130 --> 00:30:24.095
to be able to follow
the same action.

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:31.400
There are related
works in this area.

00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:36.780
So here is the imitation and
NPMP work that we just showed.

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:38.340
And there's lots of
other work that's

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:43.680
been done in demonstrating
imitation learning.

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:47.640
A lot of this happens in
model-dependent state space,

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:50.730
and often for locomotion.

00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:54.540
Existing approaches from
pixels are often applied

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:57.300
in the same domain, or where
there's a really limited domain

00:30:57.300 --> 00:30:58.380
gap.

00:30:58.380 --> 00:31:01.510
Or requires manually
labeled data, for instance,

00:31:01.510 --> 00:31:03.065
doing post-extraction.

00:31:07.250 --> 00:31:10.220
So the approach
that we have here

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:15.300
is to focus on
the representation

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:19.880
needed in order to do this
third-person visual imitation.

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:26.770
So the main properties of the
feature space we would like

00:31:26.770 --> 00:31:30.370
is that we would like to be able
to do cross domain alignment.

00:31:30.370 --> 00:31:34.720
So when we go from a real
robot to a simulated robot

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:36.140
or a real arm--

00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:39.550
a human arm to a robot
arm, from one type of robot

00:31:39.550 --> 00:31:41.650
to another type
of robot, we want

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:47.140
to be able to align those
different domain trajectories.

00:31:47.140 --> 00:31:50.890
We want the feature space to
exhibit temporal movement.

00:31:50.890 --> 00:31:55.210
And we want there to be
actionable representations,

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:58.990
meaning that the information
in that feature space

00:31:58.990 --> 00:32:04.330
is sufficient that
we can take action--

00:32:04.330 --> 00:32:06.700
the agent can take
actions with respect

00:32:06.700 --> 00:32:12.500
to that, which means
embedding the actions in that

00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:13.432
[INAUDIBLE].

00:32:18.630 --> 00:32:28.215
So we use the fact that we have
simulation, we have access to--

00:32:31.110 --> 00:32:35.640
in simulation to being able to
produce a high degree of domain

00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:38.100
randomization.

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:43.830
But moreover, we can not
only randomize the domain,

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:48.720
we can also remove
the arm entirely.

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:51.720
And you can still see
here that the block--

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:54.990
one block is being stacked
on top of another block

00:32:54.990 --> 00:32:58.210
even though the arm is
invisible all together.

00:32:58.210 --> 00:32:59.760
This is just because
they're choosing

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:01.860
not to render that in this.

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:05.130
But we can have these
aligned trajectories.

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:07.260
We can also align
trajectories where

00:33:07.260 --> 00:33:11.700
we randomize the entire domain
and where we randomized just

00:33:11.700 --> 00:33:16.440
the visual features of the arm.

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:22.410
So the difference is these
different randomized domains,

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:25.680
randomized arms,
an invisible arms

00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:27.870
are used in order
to build a data

00:33:27.870 --> 00:33:30.800
set of aligned trajectories.

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:36.210
And from there,
trained our encoder.

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:41.130
In order to train the encoder
from this data set, aligned

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:45.695
trajectory then we
use a variant of TCN,

00:33:45.695 --> 00:33:48.270
which is temporal
contrastive network--

00:33:48.270 --> 00:33:50.220
time contrastive
network, which we

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:52.875
call simple smooth
contrastive network.

00:33:55.720 --> 00:34:02.780
And we train goal condition
policies across the domains.

00:34:02.780 --> 00:34:12.190
So we want to be able to
pair trajectories and learn

00:34:12.190 --> 00:34:13.810
the right-- learn
the action that

00:34:13.810 --> 00:34:21.040
was taken in either case
across these different domains.

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:25.929
And then we can train to get
to the next goal position

00:34:25.929 --> 00:34:29.110
from the current observation
using reinforcement learning.

00:34:34.580 --> 00:34:38.250
All right, so here,
we look a little bit

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:44.719
into that feature space to
see how the distances are

00:34:44.719 --> 00:34:47.560
in the embedded space.

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:55.090
So we can take a robot
arm at the first time step

00:34:55.090 --> 00:35:01.900
and compare it to a human
hand moving through a given

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:06.120
motion over 200 time steps.

00:35:06.120 --> 00:35:09.900
And we can look at the
distances between looking

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:13.920
at cross domain case or
in the same domain case.

00:35:13.920 --> 00:35:18.270
And what we hope to see
here is that the method

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:23.640
will have a relatively
smooth, well-behaved distance

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:30.250
measure across this as it goes
from unaligned to more aligned

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:32.180
over time.

00:35:32.180 --> 00:35:34.390
So this is looking at
time contrastive network,

00:35:34.390 --> 00:35:39.340
just looking at temporarily
smooth contrastive network.

00:35:39.340 --> 00:35:45.250
This is the manipulation
and variant representation.

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:47.275
And then this is cross domain--

00:35:50.260 --> 00:35:53.020
I've forgotten what the GCP is--

00:35:53.020 --> 00:35:59.470
the generative
contrastive policy.

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:03.130
And it's basically, these
two are both ablations

00:36:03.130 --> 00:36:08.240
of the last one, manipulation
and variance representation.

00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:10.010
And so you can see
that this offers

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:14.950
a very nice smooth distance
measure in the same domain,

00:36:14.950 --> 00:36:20.830
and also offers a relatively
smooth and accurate distance

00:36:20.830 --> 00:36:22.840
measure, even when we're
looking cross domain

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:26.330
between the human
hand and a robot arm,

00:36:26.330 --> 00:36:28.690
which has not been trained for.

00:36:36.100 --> 00:36:42.050
So the goal is given
by different way points

00:36:42.050 --> 00:36:44.430
every time the video
pauses here, showing

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:47.420
that in the human hand video.

00:36:47.420 --> 00:36:50.270
Then we're giving a
new goal to the agent.

00:36:50.270 --> 00:36:53.660
And we see that then
the agent, which is this

00:36:53.660 --> 00:36:55.472
is the view that
it sees visually,

00:36:55.472 --> 00:36:56.930
and then that's
the larger picture.

00:36:56.930 --> 00:36:58.460
It's just another rendering.

00:36:58.460 --> 00:37:00.800
And we can see it following
through the same part.

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:03.140
It goes to pick
up the red block,

00:37:03.140 --> 00:37:04.810
then it hovers over
the blue block,

00:37:04.810 --> 00:37:06.800
and then places it
down and releases it

00:37:06.800 --> 00:37:10.040
at the same time
that the human hand--

00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:10.900
as it does this.

00:37:14.030 --> 00:37:15.933
So obviously, the
environment has

00:37:15.933 --> 00:37:18.350
been trained on different types
of blocks, different types

00:37:18.350 --> 00:37:20.330
of stacking and moving around.

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:22.940
But note that there is
no actual reward here

00:37:22.940 --> 00:37:25.640
that's coming from
the task of stacking.

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:33.020
The only reward is coming
from trying to get to a--

00:37:33.020 --> 00:37:34.940
get to the goal
points that are given

00:37:34.940 --> 00:37:40.180
in the video of the human hand.

00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:44.170
And no human hands were ever
seen during the actual training

00:37:44.170 --> 00:37:45.230
sequence at all.

00:37:45.230 --> 00:37:48.610
It was purely trained on--

00:37:48.610 --> 00:37:50.590
where in the three
cases where we

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:54.220
have the randomized arm,
the randomized environment

00:37:54.220 --> 00:37:56.450
and where we have
been invisible arm.

00:37:56.450 --> 00:37:59.710
So that's given us a
level of invariance

00:37:59.710 --> 00:38:01.570
in the representation
that allows

00:38:01.570 --> 00:38:06.850
us to then match the human hand,
which hasn't been seen before.

00:38:06.850 --> 00:38:09.640
And just to give a couple
more examples here.

00:38:09.640 --> 00:38:16.600
On the left, we see
tracking from a--

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:18.520
the real robot is
shown on the top.

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:23.480
And on the bottom, the
robot is shown in simulation

00:38:23.480 --> 00:38:27.410
and is stacking, again, the
red block on top of the blue.

00:38:27.410 --> 00:38:30.600
This was used just
for test purposes.

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:33.770
The middle view, we
show that the goal

00:38:33.770 --> 00:38:35.780
is being given--
the demonstration is

00:38:35.780 --> 00:38:40.220
being given using a Pick-up
Stick, but by a human.

00:38:40.220 --> 00:38:41.780
But we just see the stick.

00:38:41.780 --> 00:38:46.310
And again, the agent
is able to follow that

00:38:46.310 --> 00:38:51.130
and match that trajectory
and stack the block.

00:38:51.130 --> 00:38:56.440
And in the last view, then
we're using a different robot

00:38:56.440 --> 00:38:57.610
arm and simulation.

00:38:57.610 --> 00:39:03.100
So this demonstrating the
following a different robot--

00:39:03.100 --> 00:39:06.610
the different robot
arm-- the Jaco hand

00:39:06.610 --> 00:39:10.770
at the top and a robotic
gripper simulated at the bottom.

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:24.420
All right, next, I just have a
couple of quantitative results.

00:39:24.420 --> 00:39:29.760
We really tried to look at
this carefully and understand

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:34.950
what the benefits were of
this manipulator and varying

00:39:34.950 --> 00:39:38.230
representation.

00:39:38.230 --> 00:39:44.580
So we looked at lifting
success and stacking success

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:52.140
across having different
types of representations

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:54.090
and different types of transfer.

00:39:54.090 --> 00:39:58.740
And in each of these cases,
the most successful case

00:39:58.740 --> 00:40:04.726
is using the manipulation
and variant representation.

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:17.800
All right, so I find this
to be a nice result that

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:21.340
shows that one can use
simulation in a pretty

00:40:21.340 --> 00:40:23.650
simple way to
achieve really pretty

00:40:23.650 --> 00:40:27.250
significant invariance
over different types

00:40:27.250 --> 00:40:29.170
of representation.

00:40:29.170 --> 00:40:32.710
And this has allowed
us to have agents

00:40:32.710 --> 00:40:36.760
that can track relatively
easily how to move blocks around

00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:38.230
in this environment.

00:40:38.230 --> 00:40:41.170
Even using demonstrations from
a human from a different point

00:40:41.170 --> 00:40:49.817
of view with all of the demoing
gaps, of course, that are here.

00:40:49.817 --> 00:40:51.400
One of the key things
was in training,

00:40:51.400 --> 00:40:55.330
representation was
temporally smooth

00:40:55.330 --> 00:41:03.810
and allowed us to,
like I said, achieve

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:07.290
a level of robustness
that is a good start

00:41:07.290 --> 00:41:11.520
for third-person
visual imitation.

00:41:11.520 --> 00:41:14.720
All right, these are the
two papers that I presented.

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:16.290
The second one's on archive.

00:41:16.290 --> 00:41:18.725
The first one was
from ICML last year.

00:41:18.725 --> 00:41:21.720
Please, take a look
at those if you have--

00:41:21.720 --> 00:41:25.008
and contact the office here
if any further questions.

00:41:25.008 --> 00:41:25.550
Thanks a lot.

00:41:28.630 --> 00:41:32.450
Thanks a lot, Raia,
for your great talk.

00:41:32.450 --> 00:41:36.050
Thank you for your time and
for going into these two papers

00:41:36.050 --> 00:41:38.440
really in depth.

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:41.240
So before we get started--

00:41:41.240 --> 00:41:43.900
so basically, the
panel and the audience

00:41:43.900 --> 00:41:46.540
have many questions that
we can't wait to discuss.

00:41:46.540 --> 00:41:49.420
And before we get
started, I just

00:41:49.420 --> 00:41:53.110
wanted to mention to
the remote audience

00:41:53.110 --> 00:41:57.890
that we do have a way
for you to ask questions.

00:41:57.890 --> 00:42:01.215
So if you go to our website
page and to the livestream

00:42:01.215 --> 00:42:03.340
and you scroll down, you
see a slideshow interface.

00:42:03.340 --> 00:42:04.900
There are already
a few questions.

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:09.190
You can also just upload them
instead of asking your own.

00:42:09.190 --> 00:42:14.770
And our student panel, Krishnan
[INAUDIBLE] and Marion Lepert,

00:42:14.770 --> 00:42:16.330
will pick up your
posted questions

00:42:16.330 --> 00:42:18.260
during the discussion.

00:42:18.260 --> 00:42:21.790
But before we go
to your questions,

00:42:21.790 --> 00:42:25.040
I would like to introduce
today's guest panelists.

00:42:25.040 --> 00:42:27.460
And first, I welcome
Chelsea Finn,

00:42:27.460 --> 00:42:30.160
who is assistant professor at
the Computer Science Department

00:42:30.160 --> 00:42:32.080
at Stanford University.

00:42:32.080 --> 00:42:35.530
And I also welcome
Pulkit Agrawal,

00:42:35.530 --> 00:42:37.600
who is assistant professor
in the Department

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:41.710
of Electrical Engineering
and Computer Science at MIT.

00:42:41.710 --> 00:42:45.340
And Chelsea, I was
wondering if you

00:42:45.340 --> 00:42:49.760
can go first with any
questions you have for Raia?

00:42:49.760 --> 00:42:51.920
Yeah so I had one
lower-level comment

00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:54.080
and one higher-level comment.

00:42:54.080 --> 00:42:56.720
Or question, not comment.

00:42:56.720 --> 00:43:00.290
So for the second work,
it looked quite cool.

00:43:00.290 --> 00:43:03.260
And I was wondering,
since it's often not

00:43:03.260 --> 00:43:06.170
too hard to define a reward
function in simulation,

00:43:06.170 --> 00:43:09.260
I'm curious if you compared
to a setting where you did

00:43:09.260 --> 00:43:13.760
have rewards in simulation
instead of having human videos?

00:43:13.760 --> 00:43:17.910
Or if you have hypotheses
about how they might compare?

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:20.280
Yeah, good question.

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:22.740
It's true that we
can easily evaluate

00:43:22.740 --> 00:43:26.290
this in the simulation.

00:43:26.290 --> 00:43:29.460
And we can have those rewards.

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:32.910
And we can also look at them
on the real robot as well.

00:43:32.910 --> 00:43:36.990
In simulation, we did--

00:43:36.990 --> 00:43:41.250
so for these sets of objects,
we're able to get to--

00:43:44.730 --> 00:43:50.200
with quite a bit of training on
that specific task of stacking,

00:43:50.200 --> 00:43:59.010
we are able to get to
about 75% stacking success

00:43:59.010 --> 00:44:02.650
on the lifting,
grasping, stacking.

00:44:02.650 --> 00:44:05.350
But it does come with
the cost of having

00:44:05.350 --> 00:44:09.160
a reward that has to be--

00:44:09.160 --> 00:44:15.310
that needs to be shaped in
pieces, learning to pick up,

00:44:15.310 --> 00:44:19.510
learning to stack and
learning whether to stack red

00:44:19.510 --> 00:44:23.080
on blue or blue and red, sort of
make it conditional, et cetera.

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:27.050
So this work really
did come out of saying,

00:44:27.050 --> 00:44:30.030
this is not a sustainable way.

00:44:30.030 --> 00:44:35.850
We need to sort of escape from
the requirements of building

00:44:35.850 --> 00:44:38.030
the--

00:44:38.030 --> 00:44:41.390
of having more and
more complex task

00:44:41.390 --> 00:44:45.620
come with these insurmountable
rewards that need to be fine--

00:44:45.620 --> 00:44:49.080
need to be tuned and
trained on, et cetera.

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:54.420
So this agent can get
to, I'd say about,

00:44:54.420 --> 00:44:56.500
half of that success.

00:44:56.500 --> 00:45:01.110
But without any customized
reward or training

00:45:01.110 --> 00:45:03.950
for the task at all.

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:04.450
Great.

00:45:04.450 --> 00:45:06.320
Yeah, that makes a lot of sense.

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:08.260
And then my second
question at a high level

00:45:08.260 --> 00:45:10.570
is, at the beginning, you
argued that agents should

00:45:10.570 --> 00:45:12.340
be smaller than
their environments,

00:45:12.340 --> 00:45:14.560
such that the complexity
and richness exceeds

00:45:14.560 --> 00:45:18.040
the capacity of the agents
ability to model it.

00:45:18.040 --> 00:45:21.220
And so I'm curious what you
think about large language

00:45:21.220 --> 00:45:24.287
models, which show that--

00:45:24.287 --> 00:45:26.620
we show that basically, if
you do scale neural networks,

00:45:26.620 --> 00:45:28.828
it does seem like they can
model considerable degrees

00:45:28.828 --> 00:45:31.540
of complexity of real data.

00:45:31.540 --> 00:45:33.250
Do you think that
robotics data is

00:45:33.250 --> 00:45:36.490
more complex than the language
data that these are modeling?

00:45:36.490 --> 00:45:38.320
Do you kind of agree
with that approach

00:45:38.320 --> 00:45:40.210
or do you think that
we're ultimately going

00:45:40.210 --> 00:45:41.811
to need different approaches?

00:45:44.400 --> 00:45:46.030
It's a good question.

00:45:46.030 --> 00:45:49.190
I wish that there
was a equivalent

00:45:49.190 --> 00:45:53.000
source of natural data that
could be used for robotic.

00:45:53.000 --> 00:45:55.650
Probably the closest
thing is YouTube

00:45:55.650 --> 00:46:03.690
in terms of having large
sources of natural data

00:46:03.690 --> 00:46:10.320
that have that same level of
diversity and sort of breadth.

00:46:10.320 --> 00:46:12.690
But I don't think that
we have a straightforward

00:46:12.690 --> 00:46:17.820
way of learning robotics from
that type that type of data.

00:46:25.820 --> 00:46:30.770
But, yes, I think that GPG3
is making a lot of people,

00:46:30.770 --> 00:46:33.430
including roboticists,
think about how

00:46:33.430 --> 00:46:36.970
we can get to that
level of data collection

00:46:36.970 --> 00:46:40.900
and use so that we can train
models in that much more scaled

00:46:40.900 --> 00:46:43.446
up sort of way.

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:51.810
Yeah, thank you very
much, Chelsea and Raia,

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:53.410
for your questions.

00:46:53.410 --> 00:46:57.960
Pulkit, do you maybe have
one question for Raia?

00:46:57.960 --> 00:46:59.010
Yeah.

00:46:59.010 --> 00:47:00.410
Thanks, Raia, for the talk.

00:47:00.410 --> 00:47:02.620
It was a very good talk.

00:47:02.620 --> 00:47:05.100
So one question I have
is on the second part

00:47:05.100 --> 00:47:07.220
that you were talking
about manipulator video

00:47:07.220 --> 00:47:08.630
representation.

00:47:08.630 --> 00:47:13.080
So if for the moment I put on
my computer vision [INAUDIBLE],,

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:16.860
it seems like if he had object
representations or segmentation

00:47:16.860 --> 00:47:18.930
of objects, that
would really help

00:47:18.930 --> 00:47:21.150
us be manipulator invariant.

00:47:21.150 --> 00:47:24.300
But in the learning
community, we

00:47:24.300 --> 00:47:28.170
try to learn representations
from scratch for every task

00:47:28.170 --> 00:47:29.610
that we do.

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:31.650
And the reason is that
segmentation methods

00:47:31.650 --> 00:47:34.890
don't end up working that
well when we transfer them

00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:36.540
to our robotic tasks.

00:47:36.540 --> 00:47:40.340
So I'm wondering you know what's
your perspective going forward.

00:47:40.340 --> 00:47:43.830
I mean, should we
keep on learning

00:47:43.830 --> 00:47:45.450
doing representation learning?

00:47:45.450 --> 00:47:49.620
Or maybe try to see how we could
leverage learned segmentation

00:47:49.620 --> 00:47:53.580
models or knowledge we
collect from previous tasks

00:47:53.580 --> 00:47:56.595
so that we don't have to learn
representations for new tasks?

00:47:59.630 --> 00:48:03.470
Yeah, thanks for the
question, Pulkit.

00:48:06.570 --> 00:48:11.370
I think that it is a really
important question how

00:48:11.370 --> 00:48:15.150
we bring together computer
vision models, representation

00:48:15.150 --> 00:48:17.640
learning for the
purpose of robotics.

00:48:22.960 --> 00:48:27.030
It's not clear that it's going
to be sufficient to train

00:48:27.030 --> 00:48:29.010
in the sort of bespoke way.

00:48:29.010 --> 00:48:32.160
We need to be able to, again,
look at much wider sources

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:36.060
and data, such as I
would say YouTube again

00:48:36.060 --> 00:48:43.990
or similar type of scope and
be able to use that directly.

00:48:43.990 --> 00:48:46.045
On the other hand,
simulation does give us

00:48:46.045 --> 00:48:47.910
a tremendous amount
of power in being

00:48:47.910 --> 00:48:51.030
able to generate
things very easily

00:48:51.030 --> 00:48:55.860
and learn in a very
straightforward way.

00:48:55.860 --> 00:48:58.290
I mean, I think this is
a lot of exciting work

00:48:58.290 --> 00:49:00.090
to be done in this area.

00:49:00.090 --> 00:49:02.640
Yeah, with that,
I'm afraid we have

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:06.930
to close a little early today.

00:49:06.930 --> 00:49:09.570
And we will look
at your questions,

00:49:09.570 --> 00:49:11.700
and maybe come back to them.

00:49:11.700 --> 00:49:15.680
Thank you very much for
coming today, everyone.

